Source: homelab-postfix
Section: mail
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Jeff Gardner <mailmasker-api01@forge.name>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/permittivity2/homelab

Package: homelab-postfix
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, postfix, postfix-pgsql, adduser, postgresql-client, opendkim, opendkim-tools
Recommends: homelab-dovecot (>= 0.1.2)
Description: SMTP submission/relay wired to the homelab-* shared identity
 Wires Postfix to the shared `homelab` Postgres database for recipient
 validation only -- a narrow, column-scoped cross-schema grant (email/
 active, no password_hash at all -- see
 script/homelab-postfix-bootstrap-role) backing a virtual_mailbox_maps
 pgsql lookup that decides accept/reject at RCPT TO time. SASL
 authentication for the submission service is delegated entirely to
 Dovecot's own passdb over plain TCP (smtpd_sasl_type = dovecot,
 smtpd_sasl_path = inet:<dovecot_host>:12345) -- Postfix never queries
 Postgres for credentials itself. One Argon2id password in api.users
 backs both, but each service holds its own narrowly-scoped secret: the
 unified cross-feature identity design described in CLAUDE.md. Accepted
 mail is handed off over LMTP (port 24) to whatever host
 homelab-postfix/dovecot_host names (default localhost) -- this
 project's own production mail stack runs Postfix and Dovecot on
 separate hosts talking over exactly these same two ports, confirmed
 against the real production config, not assumed.
 .
 Like homelab-dovecot, this package owns no schema/tables of its own and
 therefore has no migrate role, unlike the standard split runtime/
 migrate Postgres role pattern most other homelab-* features use --
 an intentional, documented exception (see README.md), not an oversight.
 .
 homelab-dovecot is a Recommends, not a hard Depends: the two are
 independently placeable (see homelab-postfix/dovecot_host above) --
 installing just Postfix on a host that deliberately doesn't run
 Dovecot locally is a supported configuration, not just installable-
 but-broken.
 .
 HAProxy support (homelab-postfix/behind_haproxy, default TRUE --
 recommended, matches production) adds smtpd_upstream_proxy_protocol =
 haproxy for SMTP/submission. Answering false runs this host's SMTP/
 submission exposed with no proxy in front, an explicit opt-out rather
 than the default.
 .
 virtual_mailbox_domains is a live pgsql: lookup against
 homelab-domain-admin's domainadmin.domains table, not a single
 hardcoded domain -- `homelab-cli dns domains add/enable/disable` takes
 effect here without ever touching this host's Postfix config. Not a
 hard Depends on homelab-domain-admin (this package still installs and
 serves its originally-configured domain fine on its own); see
 README.md's "Multi-domain support" section for the rollout-safety
 mechanics if it isn't installed yet at upgrade time.
 .
 opendkim/opendkim-tools are hard Depends (the binaries are harmless to
 have installed), but actually wiring DKIM signing into the mail flow
 (smtpd_milters, starting the opendkim service) is opt-in via debconf
 (homelab-postfix/enable_dkim, default false) -- an already-configured
 host upgrading this package gets zero milter-config change unless that
 flag is explicitly turned on (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix). Key
 generation/rotation itself is homelab-domain-admin's job (`homelab-cli
 dns dkim rotate/activate/retire`) -- see its own README.md's "DKIM"
 section for the full design; this package only owns the milter wiring
 and the /etc/opendkim/keys directory permissions the `homelab` user
 needs to write new keys into.
